The capacitor alone supplies the load between peaks, so $\Delta V \approx I\,\Delta t/C$ with $\Delta t \approx 1/(2f)$ for full-wave rectification. Halving the ripple means halving $I$, halving $\Delta t$ or doubling $C$:

- **doubling $C$** (a) and **halving $I$** (b) work directly;
- **doubling the frequency** (d) halves $\Delta t$;
- going to **half-wave** (c) doubles $\Delta t$ and so doubles the ripple;
- **Schottky diodes** (e) raise the output by a few tenths of a volt but leave $I$, $\Delta t$ and $C$, and so the ripple, essentially unchanged.
